import SwiftUI

enum TracingAnalyticsStudyBank {
    static let sections: [StudySection] = [
        StudySection(id: "s1", title: "Observability Foundations", color: Color(red: 0.10, green: 0.50, blue: 0.55), lightColor: Color(red: 0.90, green: 0.96, blue: 0.97), icon: "eye.fill", objectives: [
            StudyObjective(id: "s1_o1", title: "Understand why observability is essential for AI agents and how it differs from traditional software monitoring.", topics: [
                StudyTopic(id: "s1_o1_t1", number: 1, question: "Why do AI agents require a different observability approach than deterministic software?", answer: "Traditional software is deterministic: given the same input, it produces the same output every time, making unit tests a reliable coverage mechanism. AI agents are probabilistic. The language model's response varies based on how a user phrases a request, the conversation history, the clarity of the agent's instructions, and model behavior that is not fully predictable. An agent that works flawlessly in ten test sessions can fail for the eleventh user who phrases their request slightly differently. This gap between tested behavior and real-world behavior is exactly what observability tooling is designed to close. Best practices treat observability not as an optional post-launch activity but as a core requirement of any production agent deployment."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s1_o1_t2", number: 2, question: "What are the three fundamental questions that Agentforce observability is designed to answer?", answer: "Three fundamental questions apply to every agent in production. First: Is the agent being used? This is the adoption question, answered through usage and volume analytics. Second: Is it working correctly? This is the quality question, answered through deflection rates, grounding results, and session-level traces. Third: Is it safe and healthy? This is the trust question, answered through health monitoring, safety scoring, and platform alerts. The three Agentforce observability pillars map directly to these three questions, and a complete observability strategy must address all three."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s1_o1_t3", number: 3, question: "What is the risk of operating a production agent without observability tooling?", answer: "Without observability, an agent's behavior in production is unverifiable. The language model will generate confident-sounding responses regardless of whether the underlying action executed correctly. An agent can respond 'I've updated your account' even if no record was written. An agent can present a high deflection rate on a dashboard even if many of those 'deflected' sessions ended in graceful failures rather than genuine resolution. Best practices require observability tooling to be configured before go-live so that the team operates on evidence rather than assumption from the first production session onward."),
            ]),
            StudyObjective(id: "s1_o2", title: "Identify and distinguish between the three Agentforce observability pillars.", topics: [
                StudyTopic(id: "s1_o2_t1", number: 4, question: "What is Pillar I — Agent Analytics?", answer: "The first observability pillar consists of pre-built, out-of-the-box dashboards and the underlying Data Cloud DMO tables that provide an aggregated view of how an agent performs across all sessions. Because it is not practical to manually review every conversation, analytics serve as an aggregated signal layer that surfaces trends, anomalies, and KPIs at a glance. This pillar answers the adoption question: how many users are engaging with the agent, which topics are being requested, and what percentage of sessions resolve without human escalation. It is the entry point for stakeholder reporting and early-adoption monitoring."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s1_o2_t2", number: 5, question: "What is Pillar II — Agent Optimization?", answer: "The second observability pillar provides session-level and service-level tooling for deep inspection of individual conversations and back-end execution chains. This is the layer a practitioner uses when they know something is wrong and need to identify the exact cause. It includes session trace files generated by the CLI preview tool, Agent Platform Tracing spans stored in Data Cloud, and custom evaluations that automate quality detection at scale. This pillar transforms the language model from a black box into a glass box by exposing exactly what the model received as input, what it decided to do, what data it used, and whether its response was grounded in real action output."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s1_o2_t3", number: 6, question: "What is Pillar III — Health Monitoring?", answer: "The third observability pillar provides real-time alerting built on top of the Einstein Trust Layer's fail-safe infrastructure. While analytics dashboards show what happened historically, health monitoring surfaces what is happening right now, enabling a team to respond before a systemic issue affects a large number of users. Health monitoring covers platform-level alerts for escalation rate spikes, error volume thresholds, LLM latency breaches, and session initialization failures. It also tracks integration health including Flow execution timeouts, Apex callout failures, and Data Cloud indexing lag for knowledge-grounded agents. Best practices recommend configuring all alert thresholds before a production go-live."),
            ]),
            StudyObjective(id: "s1_o3", title: "Distinguish between the two tracing systems and know when to use each one.", topics: [
                StudyTopic(id: "s1_o3_t1", number: 7, question: "What is the difference between Session Tracing and Agent Platform Tracing?", answer: "Agentforce provides two separate tracing systems at different layers. Session Tracing operates at the planner (conversational) level — it captures user input, subagent routing decisions, agent responses, and grounding results. Its primary DMO is ssot__AiAgentInteraction__dlm, and it is enabled by default with Data Cloud. Agent Platform Tracing operates at the service (back-end execution) level — it captures individual LLM calls, Flow runs, Apex invocations, duration timings, errors, and span-level attributes. Its primary DMO is ssot__TelemetryTraceSpan__dlm, and it must be explicitly enabled in Setup. Session Tracing is the conversation transcript; Platform Tracing is the execution log of every system call that powered each line of that transcript. Both are required for complete observability."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s1_o3_t2", number: 8, question: "What primary diagnostic question does each tracing system answer?", answer: "Session Tracing is the right tool when the question is 'What did the agent decide?' — it reveals which subagent handled a turn, what instructions the LLM received, what actions were available and fired, and whether the response passed grounding validation. Agent Platform Tracing is the right tool when the question is 'Why did it take that long?' or 'Where exactly did it break?' — it reveals the full hierarchical chain of back-end calls with individual span durations and status codes, enabling pinpointing a slow Apex query, a Flow returning zero rows, or an external service timeout. Conflating the two systems leads to using the wrong tool for a given diagnosis."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s1_o3_t3", number: 9, question: "How are Session Tracing and Platform Tracing joined for end-to-end analysis?", answer: "The two tracing systems are designed to be joined. The field ssot__TelemetryTraceId__c on ssot__AiAgentInteraction__dlm (Session Tracing) corresponds to the field ssot__TelemetryTrace__c on ssot__TelemetryTraceSpan__dlm (Platform Tracing). This join allows starting from a known interaction in the session trace and pulling the complete back-end execution chain that powered it. The full data hierarchy from outer to inner is: Session, then Interaction, then Step, then Span (with each Span potentially having child Spans). Best practices recommend a two-step approach: first retrieve the TelemetryTraceId from the interaction record, then query all spans matching that trace ID ordered by start time to reconstruct the execution chain."),
            ]),
        ]),
        StudySection(id: "s2", title: "Infrastructure Prerequisites", color: Color(red: 0.15, green: 0.45, blue: 0.60), lightColor: Color(red: 0.90, green: 0.95, blue: 0.98), icon: "server.rack", objectives: [
            StudyObjective(id: "s2_o1", title: "Identify and verify the infrastructure components required before any observability feature functions.", topics: [
                StudyTopic(id: "s2_o1_t1", number: 10, question: "Why is Data Cloud the mandatory foundation for all observability features?", answer: "Both Session Tracing STDM tables and Agent Platform Tracing DMOs store their data in Data Cloud. Neither feature works without Data Cloud provisioned and active in the org. A common diagnostic failure mode is finding an org where dashboards are visible but surface no data because Data Cloud was never configured. The standard verification is to query DataKnowledgeSpace — if it returns an INVALID_TYPE error, Data Cloud is not provisioned. The practitioner should direct to Setup > Data Cloud Setup and verify the CRM Connector is active before proceeding with any observability configuration."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s2_o1_t2", number: 11, question: "What is the critical no-backfill constraint for Agent Platform Tracing?", answer: "Agent Platform Tracing is disabled by default in every org and must be explicitly toggled on in Setup > Agent Platform Tracing. Span data begins populating within minutes of the first Agentforce action execution after enablement. The most important constraint: there is no retroactive backfill — any agent sessions that occurred before the toggle was enabled are permanently invisible to Platform Tracing. Best practices treat this as a critical deployment prerequisite, recommending that Platform Tracing be enabled as early as possible in every engagement, ideally before any production sessions occur."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s2_o1_t3", number: 12, question: "What permission sets are required for querying trace DMOs and why are missing permissions easy to misdiagnose?", answer: "Key permission sets include: Data Cloud Data Access for read access to DMOs, CopilotSalesforceAdmin for Agentforce Studio and agent APIs, GenieAdmin for Data Cloud administration, AgentforceServiceAgentUser for the Einstein Agent runtime user, and either GenieUserEnhancedSecurity or DataCloudUser for knowledge-grounded agents. The critical pitfall: missing permissions produce empty query results rather than error messages. A practitioner who runs a query and receives zero rows may incorrectly conclude that no data exists, when the real issue is a missing permission set. Best practices recommend verifying permission set assignments as the first step whenever a trace DMO query returns unexpectedly empty results."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s2_o1_t4", number: 13, question: "What is the infrastructure readiness checklist for a new org?", answer: "Before considering observability complete, verify: Data Cloud is provisioned and the DataKnowledgeSpace query succeeds; CopilotSalesforceAdmin permission set is assigned to the admin user; GenieAdmin permission set is assigned to the admin user; Data Cloud Data Access permission set is assigned to any user who will query DMOs; Agent Platform Tracing has been toggled on in Setup; Einstein Trust Layer Audit Trail data streams are enabled if compliance logging is required; and alert thresholds are configured before the production go-live date. Skipping any step is the most common source of 'observability is configured but surfacing no data' support issues."),
            ]),
        ]),
        StudySection(id: "s3", title: "Session-Level Tracing and Debugging", color: Color(red: 0.20, green: 0.40, blue: 0.65), lightColor: Color(red: 0.90, green: 0.94, blue: 1.0), icon: "doc.text.magnifyingglass", objectives: [
            StudyObjective(id: "s3_o1", title: "Understand how to run and interpret CLI-based session traces.", topics: [
                StudyTopic(id: "s3_o1_t1", number: 14, question: "What is the foundational rule of Agentforce debugging?", answer: "The single most important principle: the agent's text response is never evidence that an action executed. A language model will generate confident-sounding responses describing successful outcomes even when no underlying action was invoked. An agent may say 'I have created your support case' even if the FunctionStep for the case-creation action does not appear anywhere in the trace. This is an inherent property of language model generation. Best practices require verifying agent behavior by reading trace files rather than the conversation transcript. The trace is the authoritative source of truth about what actually executed."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s3_o1_t2", number: 15, question: "What is the difference between simulated preview mode and live preview mode?", answer: "Simulated mode (using --simulate-actions) generates fake action outputs with no real code executing — appropriate during early development when backing code doesn't yet exist, and avoids credit consumption. Live mode (using --use-live-actions) executes real code with real data — required for validating grounding, verifying variable-driven branching, and confirming actions return expected data shapes. Grounding failures identified in simulated mode may be false positives because LLM-generated fake outputs don't match real data patterns. Best practices recommend switching to live mode before diagnosing any grounding failure."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s3_o1_t3", number: 16, question: "How are trace files structured and how do you connect a failing turn to its trace?", answer: "Each turn generates a separate JSON trace file stored in .sfdx/agents/<AgentName>/sessions/<SESSION_ID>/traces/<PLAN_ID>.json. Three principal components: top-level metadata (planId, sessionId, detected intent, which subagent handled the turn), the plan array (ordered list of execution steps), and transcript.jsonl (links each agent turn to the planId of its corresponding trace file). To diagnose a failing turn: first read transcript.jsonl to identify the planId for that turn, then open the corresponding trace file. Traces are written after every message sent, not only at session end, so mid-session inspection is possible."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s3_o1_t4", number: 17, question: "What are the step types in an Agentforce trace and what does each reveal?", answer: "Agentforce traces contain: UserInputStep (the exact utterance), SessionInitialStateStep (full variable state at turn start), NodeEntryStateStep (which subagent is executing with state snapshot), VariableUpdateStep (variable changes with old and new values), BeforeReasoningIterationStep (confirms deterministic pre-processing fired), EnabledToolsStep (every action visible to the LLM — if expected action is absent, it cannot fire), LLMStep (full prompt sent to model, available tools, model's decision), FunctionStep (action actually executed with inputs, outputs, latency), ReasoningStep (grounding check result), TransitionStep (subagent routing), and PlannerResponseStep (final response including safety scores). Each step type has a distinct diagnostic purpose."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s3_o1_t5", number: 18, question: "Why is the LLMStep the most diagnostic step in any trace?", answer: "The LLMStep is the most valuable step because it exposes the complete input the language model received and the decision it made. The messages_sent field contains the full compiled prompt including Agent Script instructions, conversation history, variable interpolations, and platform-injected content. When an agent makes an unexpected decision, the root cause is almost always visible here. The tools_sent field lists available action names. The response_messages field shows the model's decision (natural language reply or tool invocation). The execution_latency field records LLM call duration in milliseconds — the primary diagnostic signal for voice agent latency problems where sub-2-second response is the target. Best practices: start every trace review with the LLMStep."),
            ]),
            StudyObjective(id: "s3_o2", title: "Understand grounding validation and how to prevent and diagnose grounding failures.", topics: [
                StudyTopic(id: "s3_o2_t1", number: 19, question: "What is grounding and why does it function as a quality gate?", answer: "Grounding validates that an agent's response is supported by the data returned from an action. After an action executes and returns data (in a FunctionStep), the agent composes a response. The grounding checker compares the response against the action's output. If the response contains information not traceable to the action output, the check fails with UNGROUNDED status. GROUNDED means the response accurately reflects action data. A single UNGROUNDED result causes an error message injection and a second attempt. Two consecutive UNGROUNDED results cause a terminal error message to the user. Best practices treat grounding as a core quality signal, not an edge case."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s3_o2_t2", number: 20, question: "What are the most common causes of grounding failures?", answer: "Four recurring causes: Date inference failures — an action returns a specific date but the agent responds with a relative term like 'today' that the checker cannot verify. Unit conversion failures — an action returns a precise value like '48.5F' but the agent rounds to 'about 50 degrees.' Embellishment failures — the agent adds descriptive detail not in the action output, such as commenting on weather when only temperature was returned. Loose paraphrasing failures — the agent restates output in language that diverges semantically from the source. All four share a common pattern: the agent generates content that goes beyond what the action explicitly returned."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s3_o2_t3", number: 21, question: "How do you write grounding-safe instructions to prevent failures?", answer: "The most reliable prevention is writing reasoning instructions that explicitly direct the agent to use action output values verbatim rather than paraphrasing. Unsafe pattern: vague directives like 'Tell the user about the weather.' Safe pattern: instruct the agent to use exact date and temperature values returned by the action, not paraphrase dates or round temperatures, and quote action output values directly. Explicit references using {{!@actions.ActionName}} syntax in instructions help the language model associate the correct action with the relevant response task, improving both action selection and grounding compliance."),
            ]),
            StudyObjective(id: "s3_o3", title: "Apply testing best practices for session-level validation.", topics: [
                StudyTopic(id: "s3_o3_t1", number: 22, question: "What is isolation-first debugging and why test actions before the full agent path?", answer: "When an action isn't producing expected results, test the backing Flow or Prompt Template in isolation before testing within the full agent path. The full agent path introduces confounding variables including subagent routing decisions, variable state compilation, instruction resolution, and language model non-determinism. Testing in isolation via Prompt Builder or Flow debug mode removes all of these and confirms whether the underlying code is correct before attributing the problem to agent configuration. This applies to all action types including Apex, Flow, and Prompt Template actions."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s3_o3_t2", number: 23, question: "What is bi-directional state sync validation in multi-subagent architectures?", answer: "In agents with multiple subagents, variables must flow correctly in both directions. Router-to-subagent: confirm variables set by the router appear in the child subagent's NodeEntryStateStep with correct values. Subagent-to-router: confirm variables updated by a child subagent appear in the router's VariableUpdateStep when the child completes and control returns. Skipping this validation is a common source of production failures where a child subagent has the data it needs but the router cannot access it for downstream routing or response logic."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s3_o3_t3", number: 24, question: "How should effective test utterances be written for agent preview sessions?", answer: "Test session quality depends on utterance quality. Keyword-style utterances like 'cancel' or 'order status' don't represent how real users phrase requests and can produce misleadingly positive routing results. Natural language utterances like 'I've been thinking about it and I want to cancel my subscription' or 'Can you look up where my package is? Order number is 12345' reflect actual user phrasing patterns and expose routing edge cases, instruction gaps, and grounding vulnerabilities that keyword tests miss. Best practices require test utterances to be phrased as complete natural language sentences before a go-live decision is made."),
            ]),
        ]),
        StudySection(id: "s4", title: "Agent Platform Tracing", color: Color(red: 0.30, green: 0.35, blue: 0.60), lightColor: Color(red: 0.92, green: 0.93, blue: 1.0), icon: "chart.bar.doc.horizontal.fill", objectives: [
            StudyObjective(id: "s4_o1", title: "Understand how Platform Tracing works at the service level and how to interpret trace trees.", topics: [
                StudyTopic(id: "s4_o1_t1", number: 25, question: "How does Agent Platform Tracing capture back-end execution as a hierarchical trace tree?", answer: "Platform Tracing stores every action execution as an OpenTelemetry trace tree in Data Cloud. A single user utterance can trigger a cascade of downstream calls including LLM steps, Flow executions, Apex invocations, and external service callouts. Platform Tracing captures all of these as a hierarchical tree of spans, where each span has a status code (OK or ERROR) and records duration in milliseconds. Parent-child relationships are reconstructed by matching ssot__TelemetryParentSpanId__c to another span's ssot__Id__c. Spans with null or zeroed parent ID are root spans. This tree structure shows not just that something failed but exactly which step caused the failure and how long each step took."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s4_o1_t2", number: 26, question: "What are the key fields on ssot__TelemetryTraceSpan__dlm and their diagnostic purpose?", answer: "Key fields: ssot__Id__c (unique span identifier for reconstructing parent-child relationships); ssot__TelemetryParentSpanId__c (parent span, null/zeroed for root spans); ssot__TelemetryTrace__c (trace ID joining to Session Tracing via ssot__TelemetryTraceId__c on ssot__AiAgentInteraction__dlm); ssot__OperationName__c (operation type such as run.llmstep, run.action.X, or run.invokeActions.FLOW); ssot__StatusCode__c (OK or ERROR); ssot__DurationNumber__c (duration in milliseconds); ssot__TelemetrySpanAttributeText__c (key-value attributes including flow.api.name, db.rows_affected, and db.operation.name — essential for diagnosing data volume problems)."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s4_o1_t3", number: 27, question: "Why do span attributes reveal diagnostic detail that the operation name does not?", answer: "The operation name on a span does not always accurately describe what happened. A span named with a 'create record' pattern may, when its db.operation.name attribute is inspected, reveal it performed a query instead of an insert. A Flow action that completed with OK status may show db.rows_affected=0 in its attributes, indicating the underlying query returned no results even though the Flow itself didn't throw an exception. Without span attributes, a practitioner may assume an insert failed when actually a lookup returned no matching data, leading to incorrect remediation. Best practices require reading span attributes alongside status codes and operation names."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s4_o1_t4", number: 28, question: "What are the core query patterns for performance profiling using Platform Tracing data?", answer: "Standard query patterns include: Performance-by-operation-type groups spans by ssot__OperationName__c and aggregates average and maximum duration along with span count, ordered by descending average duration — the first query to run when an agent 'feels slow.' Error spans query filters by ssot__StatusCode__c = 'ERROR' to retrieve recent errors, and a count-by-operation-type variant identifies which steps are most failure-prone. Full trace reconstruction filters by a specific ssot__TelemetryTrace__c value and orders by ssot__StartDateTime__c ascending, producing the complete execution chain for a single interaction."),
            ]),
            StudyObjective(id: "s4_o2", title: "Understand Slackbot integration as a conversational interface for trace analysis.", topics: [
                StudyTopic(id: "s4_o2_t1", number: 29, question: "How can Slackbot be used for natural language trace queries?", answer: "Because Platform Tracing data lives in Data Cloud, it can be queried by connected systems including Slackbot. A practical implementation connects query patterns to a Slack canvas and exposes it to Slackbot, enabling non-technical team members to ask trace analysis questions in natural language and receive structured trace tree results without writing queries directly. This pattern is valuable in enterprise environments where operations managers need trace visibility but aren't query practitioners. The Slackbot connection requires Agent Platform Tracing to be enabled, and the Slackbot must be provided with DMO schemas, sample query patterns, and join patterns via the Slack canvas at the start of each diagnostic session. This is a manual configuration step."),
            ]),
        ]),
        StudySection(id: "s5", title: "MCP Actions and Observability Boundaries", color: Color(red: 0.40, green: 0.30, blue: 0.55), lightColor: Color(red: 0.94, green: 0.92, blue: 1.0), icon: "network", objectives: [
            StudyObjective(id: "s5_o1", title: "Understand how observability behavior changes when agents use MCP external actions.", topics: [
                StudyTopic(id: "s5_o1_t1", number: 30, question: "How do MCP actions differ from native Salesforce actions at the observability level?", answer: "MCP servers are registered external systems whose tools appear as callable actions in an Agentforce agent. From the agent's perspective they are invocable like native actions, but the execution chain differs: instead of a Salesforce-managed invocation with full instrumentation, the call goes to an external HTTP endpoint. Session Tracing records the invocation in a FunctionStep, but output detail depends on the MCP server's own logging. Platform Tracing captures spans only to the HTTP boundary; internal MCP server execution is opaque to Salesforce instrumentation. Error attribution and latency profiling are limited to what can be observed at the network boundary."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s5_o1_t2", number: 31, question: "How do you identify MCP action spans in a Platform Tracing trace tree?", answer: "In a trace tree, MCP-backed actions have a specific span pattern. Native Apex or Flow actions show child spans under the invocation span with detailed sub-steps and db.* attributes. MCP actions instead show a span for the action invocation followed by a span for run.invokeActions.EXTERNAL_SERVICE with no child spans beneath it. The absence of child spans under the external service invocation is the diagnostic signature of an MCP or external HTTP call — the trace tree stops at the HTTP boundary because Salesforce instrumentation cannot reach inside the external system."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s5_o1_t3", number: 32, question: "How do you diagnose latency in MCP-backed agent actions?", answer: "Because Platform Tracing cannot observe internal MCP execution, latency profiling requires a two-part approach. Part one measures the HTTP boundary: query ssot__TelemetryTraceSpan__dlm filtered by the MCP action's operation name and use ssot__DurationNumber__c for the full round-trip time. Part two, if available, compares span duration against the MCP server's own instrumentation logs to separate network latency from server processing time. Without server-side instrumentation, span duration is an upper bound only. For chained MCP callouts, each hop compounds latency — two hops averaging 400ms each can produce a 95th-percentile total exceeding the sub-2-second voice agent response threshold, which is an architectural concern to flag."),
            ]),
        ]),
        StudySection(id: "s6", title: "Dashboards and Analytics", color: Color(red: 0.25, green: 0.50, blue: 0.45), lightColor: Color(red: 0.91, green: 0.96, blue: 0.95), icon: "chart.bar.xaxis", objectives: [
            StudyObjective(id: "s6_o1", title: "Identify the out-of-the-box dashboard surfaces and the metrics each provides.", topics: [
                StudyTopic(id: "s6_o1_t1", number: 33, question: "What are the primary metrics on the Agentforce Analytics Dashboard?", answer: "The primary out-of-the-box dashboard includes: Conversation Volume (daily and weekly session counts for adoption curve monitoring), Topic and Intent Breakdown (which subagents are invoked most frequently, revealing demand patterns and potential mis-routing), Session Duration (average turn count and conversation length as a proxy for resolution complexity), Escalation Rate (percentage of sessions handed off to a human agent), Deflection Rate (percentage resolved without human escalation), Sub-agent Invocation Count (per-subagent usage trends), and End-to-End Workflow Resolution (full session success rate over rolling periods). The Escalation Rate is identified as the primary quality signal in the first two weeks after a production launch."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s6_o1_t2", number: 34, question: "What do the Consumption Analytics and Consumption Insights dashboards provide?", answer: "Two additional dashboards focus on credit and token consumption rather than quality. The Consumption Analytics Dashboard (powered by Tableau Next) provides: Daily Active Users per agent per day, Input vs Output Token Breakdown as a cost optimization signal, Token Usage by User identifying heaviest consumers and anomalies, and Usage by Agent for budget attribution. The Consumption Insights Dashboard (powered by Data 360 Reporting) provides more granular per-feature and per-model token breakdowns, appropriate for teams managing Einstein credit budgets across multiple AI products. Neither surfaces quality or routing information — they are cost and adoption tools."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s6_o1_t3", number: 35, question: "Why is the escalation rate the most important early-adoption metric?", answer: "The escalation rate is the primary quality signal in the first one to two weeks because test utterances cannot fully represent real user phrasing diversity. Real users introduce variations, abbreviations, multi-intent messages, and contextual assumptions that tests rarely surface. A spike in escalation rate is the first concrete signal that a category of real user intent is not being handled correctly, arriving before users complain through support channels or abandon the channel. Best practices recommend monitoring escalation rate daily for the first two weeks and investigating any increase greater than 20% above baseline by pulling trace files from escalated sessions to identify the common instruction gap or missing subagent coverage."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s6_o1_t4", number: 36, question: "What is the risk of interpreting raw deflection rate as genuine resolution?", answer: "Raw deflection rate counts all sessions that ended without escalation as deflected, including sessions where the agent responded with a graceful failure message like 'I cannot find your order, please contact support.' Sessions ending without escalation are classified as deflected even when the agent failed to resolve the user's need. This creates an inflated metric giving false confidence. Best practices recommend building a quality-corrected deflection measure by identifying sessions where error steps occurred and cross-referencing against non-escalated sessions. A more robust approach uses a custom 'resolved' variable that the agent sets only when genuine resolution is achieved, then filters the deflection metric to count only sessions where that variable is true."),
            ]),
            StudyObjective(id: "s6_o2", title: "Understand the Data Cloud STDM table model for building custom analytics.", topics: [
                StudyTopic(id: "s6_o2_t1", number: 37, question: "What are the core STDM tables and what does each contain?", answer: "Core tables: ssot__AiAgentSession__dlm holds session-level data (start time, end time, session ID). ssot__AiAgentInteraction__dlm holds interaction metadata (intent, subagent, resolution status, TelemetryTraceId for joining to Platform Tracing). ssot__AiAgentInteractionMessage__dlm holds user and agent message content for conversation analysis. ssot__AiAgentInteractionStep__dlm holds action steps with error tracking. ssot__AiAgentSessionParticipant__dlm records agent participation. AiAgentGenerativeAiUsage_std__dlm records token usage per session. ssot__TelemetryTraceSpan__dlm holds back-end execution spans from Platform Tracing. Custom dashboards on these tables surface insights unavailable in any out-of-the-box panel."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s6_o2_t2", number: 38, question: "What are the Einstein Trust Layer Audit Trail DMO tables?", answer: "The Trust Layer generates audit trail DMOs: GenAIGatewayRequest__dlm captures the compiled prompt sent to the LLM gateway (not the user's raw utterance — that comes from ssot__AiAgentInteractionMessage__dlm where role equals 'user'). GenAIGatewayResponse__dlm captures the raw LLM response. GenAIGeneration__dlm holds generation metadata per LLM call. GenAIFeedback__dlm records user feedback signals (thumbs up/down ratings). An important distinction: GenAIGatewayRequest__dlm captures the compiled Agent Script prompt, not the user's original utterance. These tables only populate when their corresponding data streams are explicitly enabled in Einstein Trust Layer settings."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s6_o2_t3", number: 39, question: "What are the six categories of proactive monitoring alerts?", answer: "Six categories: Agent Rate Limit Errors fire when the org-level LLM rate limit is reached, indicating delays at peak load. Voice Agent Response Failures fire on TTS/STT pipeline error spikes, signaling a voice channel SLA breach. Planner Session Start Failures fire when the agent cannot initialize sessions, indicating complete channel outage. LLM Model Latency alerts fire when response time exceeds a threshold — critical for voice agents where sub-2-second is the target. Agent Escalation Rate alerts fire at more than 10 fallbacks per 5-minute window, indicating systemic routing failure. Error Volume alerts fire at more than 25 errors per 5-minute window, indicating platform-level issues. All thresholds are configurable and should be calibrated relative to expected traffic volume before go-live."),
            ]),
        ]),
        StudySection(id: "s7", title: "Common Diagnostic Patterns", color: Color(red: 0.55, green: 0.25, blue: 0.35), lightColor: Color(red: 0.96, green: 0.91, blue: 0.93), icon: "stethoscope", objectives: [
            StudyObjective(id: "s7_o1", title: "Apply a systematic diagnostic approach to the most common Agentforce agent failure modes.", topics: [
                StudyTopic(id: "s7_o1_t1", number: 40, question: "How do you diagnose Pattern A: Wrong subagent routing?", answer: "This pattern presents when a user asks about one topic but the agent routes to an incorrect subagent. Start at the LLMStep for the routing turn, checking tools_sent to confirm all transition actions are listed. If a transition action is absent from tools_sent, its 'available when' condition is blocking it. If all transition actions are visible but the wrong one was selected, the subagent descriptions are ambiguous or overlap. Add disambiguation language that explicitly excludes the topic handled by the other subagent — exclusive phrasing on each description is more reliable than relying on the model to infer the boundary from inclusive descriptions alone."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s7_o1_t2", number: 41, question: "How do you diagnose Pattern B: Action not firing despite being expected?", answer: "First check the EnabledToolsStep — if the expected action is absent, the action's 'available when' guard isn't being met. Cross-reference the guard condition against the NodeEntryStateStep to see which variable values were present and identify which condition failed. If the action is listed in enabled tools but wasn't called, the action's description doesn't match the user's phrasing closely enough for model recognition. Fix: relax or restructure the guard condition (first case), or strengthen the action description with trigger keywords from actual utterances (second case)."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s7_o1_t3", number: 42, question: "How do you diagnose Pattern C: Behavioral loop repeating the same question?", answer: "Examine repeated LLMStep entries across consecutive turns and check messages_sent for the instruction triggering the repeated question. The typical root cause is an instruction evaluating a variable condition rather than checking conversation history. When the variable hasn't been explicitly set by a prior action, the condition always evaluates as true and the question fires again regardless of what the user said. Restructure instructions to treat conversation history as the authoritative source, and use slot-filling patterns with the '...' input syntax rather than requiring explicit variable assignment."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s7_o1_t4", number: 43, question: "How do you diagnose Pattern D: Terminal 'unexpected error' response?", answer: "The 'I apologize, but I encountered an unexpected error' message fires only when grounding fails twice consecutively. Find the consecutive pair of ReasoningStep entries both showing UNGROUNDED status and examine the preceding FunctionStep. Two root causes: (1) the action executed successfully but the model embellished or paraphrased beyond what the data supports — fix with verbatim-usage instructions; (2) the action returned an error or empty data, leaving nothing to ground against — fix the backing code to return valid data rather than throwing exceptions on edge-case inputs."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s7_o1_t5", number: 44, question: "How do you diagnose Pattern E: Action returns data but response is generic?", answer: "A FunctionStep shows successful execution with data, but the agent's reply is generic. Examine the LLMStep following the FunctionStep — if the model invoked a different tool or generated a response without referencing the action output, the instructions don't explicitly direct the model to use specific output fields. Update reasoning instructions to name specific fields by reference using {{!@actions.ActionName.fieldName}} syntax, creating an explicit connection between action output and expected response content. Generic responses ignoring action data are almost always an instruction authoring gap, not a data retrieval failure."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s7_o1_t6", number: 45, question: "How do you diagnose Pattern F: Latency complaint with no visible errors?", answer: "The agent responds successfully, actions complete with OK status, no alerts fired — but users report slowness. This requires Agent Platform Tracing. Query ssot__TelemetryTraceSpan__dlm with the performance-by-operation-type query to identify which operation type has elevated average duration. Drill into span attributes, specifically db.rows_affected and db.operation.name. A high db.rows_affected value (e.g., several thousand rows on what should be a targeted lookup) indicates the backing query is unfiltered or missing a LIMIT clause. The model then processes a large dataset, creating downstream LLM latency that traces record as success while Platform Tracing reveals the data volume problem."),
            ]),
            StudyObjective(id: "s7_o2", title: "Use a structured fix reference to match symptoms to remediation strategies.", topics: [
                StudyTopic(id: "s7_o2_t1", number: 46, question: "What is the fix strategy matrix for mapping symptoms to configuration targets?", answer: "The fix mapping: Subagent not matched → update the subagent's description field with trigger keywords from the failing utterance. Action not invoked → make the action's description in reasoning.actions more trigger-specific. Action blocked by guard → relax or restructure the 'available when' condition to match the variable state present when the action should be invocable. Two actions overlap → update both description fields with mutual exclusion language. Grounding failure (UNGROUNDED) → add explicit output references in reasoning: instructions: -> block. Low safety score → add explicit safety guidelines and scope boundary definitions in system: instructions block."),
            ]),
        ]),
        StudySection(id: "s8", title: "Sandbox vs. Production Observability", color: Color(red: 0.35, green: 0.45, blue: 0.50), lightColor: Color(red: 0.93, green: 0.95, blue: 0.96), icon: "arrow.left.arrow.right.square.fill", objectives: [
            StudyObjective(id: "s8_o1", title: "Understand the differences in tracing approach between sandbox and production environments.", topics: [
                StudyTopic(id: "s8_o1_t1", number: 47, question: "What are the key differences between sandbox and production observability configuration?", answer: "Observability configuration is not automatically inherited from sandbox to production. In sandbox, the primary tracing tool is the CLI preview generating local trace files that are ephemeral and practical for manual review at low volume. In production, primary surfaces are Data Cloud STDM tables and Platform Tracing DMOs requiring queries — the only scalable approach at high session volume. Platform Tracing must be enabled separately in each org. Alert thresholds are not typically configured in sandbox but must be configured before production go-live. Credit consumption occurs in both environments for live action execution — simulated mode is the only way to avoid costs during iterative development."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s8_o1_t2", number: 48, question: "How can Platform Tracing be deployed via metadata instead of manual Setup steps?", answer: "Agent Platform Tracing can be enabled as a metadata deployment via the AgentforcePlatformTracingSettings custom metadata type with an isEnabled field. Deploying this metadata in the same pipeline stage as agent metadata ensures Platform Tracing is always enabled in target environments without depending on a manual step that may be missed. This is consistent with best practices for configuration that affects observability: if a setting affects production behavior, it should be version-controlled and deployed as metadata rather than configured ad-hoc."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s8_o1_t3", number: 49, question: "What is the recommended sequence for transitioning from sandbox to production monitoring?", answer: "Best practices recommend: (1) Verify Platform Tracing is enabled in production by confirming the metadata deployment or Setup toggle. (2) Confirm Data Cloud DMO accessibility by running the DataKnowledgeSpace verification query and checking permission set assignments. (3) Seed baseline metrics during the first 48 hours of production traffic — dashboards need real data before alerts can be meaningfully calibrated. (4) Configure all alert thresholds relative to expected traffic volume before launch. (5) Transition the team from individual local trace file review to query-based STDM queries as the primary diagnostic workflow. The sandbox habit of opening individual trace files does not scale at production volumes of hundreds or thousands of sessions per day."),
            ]),
        ]),
        StudySection(id: "s9", title: "Business Value and Cost Management", color: Color(red: 0.45, green: 0.40, blue: 0.25), lightColor: Color(red: 0.96, green: 0.95, blue: 0.91), icon: "chart.line.uptrend.xyaxis", objectives: [
            StudyObjective(id: "s9_o1", title: "Translate technical observability metrics into business KPIs and executive-facing outcomes.", topics: [
                StudyTopic(id: "s9_o1_t1", number: 50, question: "How do you map technical metrics to business KPIs for executive reporting?", answer: "Key mappings: Deflection rate from ssot__AiAgentSession__dlm maps to cost-per-interaction reduction. Escalation rate maps to agent quality and user trust for CX leadership. Action span duration from ssot__TelemetryTraceSpan__dlm maps to time-to-resolution and UX quality. Error volume by operation type maps to IT operations efficiency and MTTR. Token usage per session from AiAgentGenerativeAiUsage_std__dlm maps to AI cost-per-transaction for finance. Safety score distribution from PlannerResponseStep.safetyScore maps to brand risk and compliance posture. User feedback from GenAIFeedback__dlm maps to NPS and user satisfaction for CX leadership."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s9_o1_t2", number: 51, question: "How do you build a quantitative ROI case using observability data?", answer: "Five steps: (1) Establish the human cost baseline by querying historical case volume and average handle time. (2) Measure genuine deflection rate using a quality-corrected query — counting only sessions where an action executed successfully and no escalation occurred. (3) Calculate cost avoidance by multiplying genuine deflections by the difference between human cost per interaction and AI credit cost per session. (4) Use Platform Tracing latency data to surface efficiency gains from action optimization, expressed as reductions in time-to-resolution. (5) Track error span trends over time to demonstrate reliability improvements. These five data points constitute a defensible ROI narrative built entirely from observability data."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s9_o1_t3", number: 52, question: "How does latency connect to cost-per-interaction as a business metric?", answer: "Action span duration is both a performance metric and a cost driver. In voice agent deployments where sub-2-second response is the threshold for natural conversation, sessions exceeding this have measurably higher probability of escalation or abandonment. An abandoned interaction escalating to a human costs significantly more than a genuinely deflected one. Quantify this by pulling the distribution of span durations, identifying sessions above the 2,000-millisecond threshold, and cross-referencing against escalation status. If strong correlation exists between above-threshold latency and escalation, each 100-millisecond reduction translates to measurable decrease in escalation rate and corresponding cost reduction."),
            ]),
            StudyObjective(id: "s9_o2", title: "Understand credit consumption patterns and apply cost-aware debugging practices.", topics: [
                StudyTopic(id: "s9_o2_t1", number: 53, question: "What are the credit consumption rules by operation type?", answer: "Framework operations are free: @utils.transition, @utils.setVariables, @utils.escalate, if/else control flow, before_reasoning/after_reasoning hooks — all execute deterministically without LLM involvement. The LLM reasoning turn itself is also free; credits apply to actions invoked as a result of reasoning. Flow actions cost 20 credits per execution. Apex actions cost 20 credits per execution. Any other action type also costs 20 credits. Prompt Template actions cost 2-16 credits depending on complexity. A session with five action executions costs 100 credits in live mode. Simulated preview avoids these costs because no real actions execute."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s9_o2_t2", number: 54, question: "What are cost-aware debugging practices to minimize credit expenditure?", answer: "Use simulated mode for all instruction authoring and routing iteration since it generates fake outputs at no credit cost. Reserve live preview for grounding validation, variable-driven branching verification, and final behavioral testing before go-live. An escalation spike alert at >10 fallbacks per 5 minutes represents both a UX problem and unplanned credit consumption if the fallback path involves action retries — each failed action call still costs 20 credits. Actions exceeding Apex governor limits appear as FunctionStep errors and represent 20 credits consumed per failed call. Optimizing actions to stay within governor limits has both performance and cost benefits."),
            ]),
        ]),
        StudySection(id: "s10", title: "Roadmap Awareness and Stakeholder Communication", color: Color(red: 0.50, green: 0.35, blue: 0.40), lightColor: Color(red: 0.96, green: 0.93, blue: 0.94), icon: "megaphone.fill", objectives: [
            StudyObjective(id: "s10_o1", title: "Distinguish between currently available features and roadmap items, and communicate limitations accurately.", topics: [
                StudyTopic(id: "s10_o1_t1", number: 55, question: "What observability features are currently available in production?", answer: "Currently available: out-of-the-box Tableau Next dashboards for consumption and agent analytics; session-level trace files via the CLI preview; Agent Platform Tracing via ssot__TelemetryTraceSpan__dlm requiring manual enablement; Data Cloud STDM tables for custom query-based dashboards; Einstein Trust Layer audit trail DMOs when data streams are explicitly enabled; safety scoring on every agent response via PlannerResponseStep.safetyScore; and Slackbot integration for natural language trace queries requiring manual canvas configuration. Practitioners should verify features referenced in stakeholder conversations are confirmed available in the current release."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s10_o1_t2", number: 56, question: "What capabilities are on the roadmap and what are the current workarounds?", answer: "Roadmap items with current workarounds: Error Rate Metric Splitting (approximated by manually querying and categorizing errors from ssot__AiAgentInteractionStep__dlm). Latency Split Tracking (approximated by extracting ssot__DurationNumber__c from individual spans). Custom Evaluations using LLM-as-a-Judge for semantic conflict detection (requires custom queries with manual pattern detection). Spend and consumption reporting within agent analytics (approximated by cross-referencing AiAgentGenerativeAiUsage_std__dlm with session data). Sentiment analysis and RAG quality metrics are not available without custom external pipelines."),
                StudyTopic(id: "s10_o1_t3", number: 57, question: "How should roadmap limitations be framed in stakeholder conversations?", answer: "Effective framing: (1) Acknowledge what is available today with specificity — real-time escalation rate monitoring, session-level execution traces, and back-end performance profiling down to individual Apex and Flow invocations. (2) Acknowledge what is coming without committing to a delivery timeline — granular error categorization and latency split reporting by subagent are on the roadmap. (3) Bridge the gap — custom queries against the STDM tables can approximate both capabilities today while native implementation arrives. This approach maintains credibility with technical stakeholders while setting realistic expectations for what requires custom development versus what will eventually be configuration."),
            ]),
        ]),
    ]
}
